  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ショウブ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%B7%E3%83%A7%E3%82%A6%E3%83%96",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 110084610,
      "text": "ショウブ は、池、川などに生える多年生の草本で、単子葉植物の一種。APG体系ではショウブ目ショウブ科のショウブ属に属する。ユーラシア大陸から北米大陸の温帯から暖帯に広く分布し、日本を含めて東アジアのものは変種 A. calamus var. angustatusとされる。薬草、漢方薬としても用いられている。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Acorus calamus」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acorus_calamus",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1362096137,
      "text": "Acorus calamus is a species of flowering plant. It is a tall wetland monocot of the family Acoraceae, in the genus Acorus. Although used in traditional medicine over centuries to treat digestive disorders and pain, it has no clinical evidence of safety or efficacy and may be toxic if ingested, and so has been commercially banned for use in food in the United States."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Acorus calamus」の節「Horticulture」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acorus_calamus#Horticulture",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1362096137,
        "heading": "Horticulture",
        "text": "This plant is sometimes used as a pond plant in horticulture. There is at least one tetraploid ornamental cultivar known; it is usually called 'Variegatus', but the RHS recommends calling it 'Argenteostriatus'.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    },
    "toxicity": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Acorus calamus」の節「Toxicity」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acorus_calamus#Toxicity",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1362096137,
        "heading": "Toxicity",
        "text": "Although calamus has been used for its fragrance and ingested, it has not been studied by rigorous clinical research. Individual medical reports of toxicity mention severe nausea and prolonged vomiting over many hours following oral uses. Laboratory studies of its extracts indicate other forms of toxicity, due mainly to the emetic compound β-asarone. In Ayurveda, the rhizome is used only after a purification step (shodhana),\nin which it is boiled in media such as cow's milk. Analytical studies have\nreported that this processing significantly reduces the rhizome's β-asarone\ncontent, an effect attributed largely to\nvolatilization of the heat-labile compound during prolonged boiling.\nAllegedly, the plant is psychoactive (hallucinogenic), but for example all experiments with American calamus have been completely unsuccessful, even those involving very high dosages (up to 300 g of rhizomes).",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
